## Session 4 - Round 18+ (claude41-50): Assembly-Guided Optimization

### Key Discovery: Accumulator Spilling in Assembly
Assembly analysis of claude13_1 (best matmul_1) revealed that the 6×8×1 BLIS micro-kernel
stores ALL 12 accumulator YMM values back to C memory after EVERY K iteration, then reloads
them. Per unrolled K-step:
- 2 vmovupd loads for B columns
- 6 vbroadcastsd loads for A elements
- 12 vfmadd FMA instructions
- 12 vmovupd STORES back to C memory (should stay in registers!)

In MKL's hand-tuned assembly, accumulators stay in registers for the entire K loop.
This is caused by transfer_to_scf converting vector transfers to scalar SCF loops that
LLVM cannot optimize into register-resident accumulators.

